<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Martel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 688–741 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Martel was a Frankish military leader and mayor of the palace who effectively ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a> in the early eighth century. He defeated an Umayyad army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tours">Battle of Tours</a> in 732 CE. His power laid the foundations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a>, and he was the grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-martel.jpg" alt="Charles Martel" width="320" height="259" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Martel_divise_le_royaume_entre_P%C3%A9pin_et_Carloman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles was a son of the Frankish statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-of-herstal">Pepin of Herstal</a> and a noblewoman named Alpaida, who was his mistress and possibly his second wife. Older writing calls him illegitimate, but the line between wives and concubines was not clear-cut in 8th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a>, and the charge probably comes from the wish of Pepin's first wife Plectrude to see her own line as heirs. By his lifetime the Merovingian kings had ceded power to the mayors of the palace, who controlled the treasury and granted land in the name of a figurehead king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Pepin died in December 714, Plectrude had designated his young grandson Theudoald as sole heir and imprisoned Charles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. Charles escaped in 715 and was acclaimed mayor of the palace over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>. In the civil war that followed he suffered his only defeat, near Cologne in 716, against the Neustrians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/redbad">Radbod, King of the Frisians</a>. He then won at Amblève in April 716 and at Vinchy on 21 March 717, took Cologne, and defeated Chilperic II and Odo, duke of Aquitaine, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a> in 718. Between 718 and 732 he campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>, the Frisians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and the Alemanni, and he appointed the Merovingian kings himself.</p><p>In 731 he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> into Aquitaine. An army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-al-ghafiqi">Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi</a> then marched north and was defeated by Charles at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tours">Battle of Tours</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, in 732. The historian Paul Fouracre suggests the Arab campaign was a long-distance raid rather than the start of a war, and Arabic sources of the period give the battle little prominence. Charles did not gain full control of Aquitaine, and Odo remained duke until 735. After the death of King Theuderic IV in 737 he appointed no successor, and the throne stayed vacant until his own death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charles died on 22 October 741 at Quierzy-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oise">Oise</a> and was buried at Saint Denis. A year earlier he had divided his lands between his sons Carloman and Pepin the Younger. Many historians see him as laying the foundations for Pepin's rise to the Frankish throne in 751 and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s imperial acclamation in 800, although Fouracre notes that his career ended with unfinished business.</p><p>His reputation has varied. Early authors praised his victories, while a 9th-century text portrayed him suffering in hell for taking church lands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon">Edward Gibbon</a> and later writers made him the saviour of Christian Europe at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, a view that more recent historians such as Alessandro Barbero call overstated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles Martel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pepin of Herstal, <b>Charles Martel</b> and Pepin the Short turned the Carolingians from palace mayors into the effective rulers of the Frankish kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The mayors of the palace restored it by force: <b>Charles Martel</b> defeated an Umayyad army under Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi between Poitiers and Tours, usually dated 732, and his son Pippin the Short subdued Aquitaine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They were long a match for the Franks: in the 550s they inflicted a heavy defeat on Chlothar I, and in the 8th century they resisted repeated campaigns by <b>Charles Martel</b> and Pippin the Short.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Raids beyond the Pyrenees were checked by <b>Charles Martel</b> at the Battle of Tours (Poitiers) in 732.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles Martel</b>, the Frankish mayor of the palace who died in 741, was the first to reward his followers with lands on a large scale, much of it taken from the Church, and the German historian Heinrich Brunner argued in 1887 that he did so to pay for…</blockquote><a href="/government/feudalism">Feudalism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>After a struggle, his son <b>Charles Martel</b> took control and laid the ground for the Carolingian dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-16">16 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herstal">Herstal</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in Quierzy, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.66,5.63&amp;year=722" aria-label="Herstal on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4678 405 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles Martel"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5128 675h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5128 675h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.66,5.63&amp;year=722">What was here in 722 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
